{
  "__comment": "Chain ingestion rules. A chain reacts to a committed write on trigger_table and projects it onto target_table through a mapper. | The production->inventory rule was removed with the demo tables it referenced (2026-08-01 fixture reset).",
  "_max_chain_depth_comment": "How deep a chain may wake another chain. An event past this depth is refused by name and ends FAILED, so nothing behind it is blocked. An event from outside a chain has no depth and is not affected.",
  "max_chain_depth": 8,
  "rules": [
    {
      "__comment": "dt_log -> dt_map: the DT map is DERIVED. dt_log is the record; the map is a projection of it, so nothing should ever write dt_map by hand -- a replay would then silently disagree with the hand-written rows. | SHIPS DISABLED and stays disabled this round by instruction. What was measured (2026-08-01): a chain CAN address a single map cell -- crud.apply_batch_updates composes the composite business key from the update payload itself (crud.py:1051-1059) when the caller omits business_key_val and supplies every composite_key_source column, and dt_map's composite key includes dt_x/dt_y, so one business key = one cell. What a chain CANNOT do is PURGE: replace_map lives on the batch (schemas.py:138) and chain_ingestion_worker.py:437-441 constructs the batch itself and never sets it, so a chain can only upsert cells and never delete stale ones. That is the open question, and it is why this stays off.",
      "name": "dt_log_to_dt_map",
      "derivation_source_table": "dt_log",
      "trigger_table": "dt_log",
      "target_table": "dt_map",
      "mapper_module": "mappers.dt_map_mapper",
      "mapper_function": "build_dt_map_batch_df",
      "is_batch": true,
      "enabled": false
    },
    {
      "__comment": "DT frame metadata: the source map is dt_log itself, one metadata row per dt_job. The mapper calls the same read-only alignment-view service as the UI, accepts only a declared index-axis winner, and writes the full projected grid_metadata through normal chain priority (so user metadata remains higher priority). dt_job ingestion is one transaction, hence this batch trigger sees the completed job; incomplete decision keys are intentionally a no-op. dt_map is NOT the target here: it is the later standardized projection.",
      "name": "dt_log_to_dt_alignment_metadata",
      "trigger_table": "dt_log",
      "target_table": "wafer_map_metadata",
      "mapper_module": "mappers.dt_alignment_metadata_mapper",
      "mapper_function": "build_dt_alignment_metadata_batch",
      "is_batch": true,
      "alignment_rule": "dt_frame_confrimation",
      "map_table": "dt_log",
      "metadata_target_table": "dt_log",
      "reference_job_column": "dt_job",
      "geometry_bootstrap": "reference_only",
      "reference_by_job_pattern": [
        {
          "contains": "SYN",
          "reference_spec": "valid_die_ref:PRD-A_DT13"
        }
      ],
      "enabled": false
    },
    {
      "__comment": "BLOCKED 2026-09-06, NOT by its own condition any more. The job column it asked for IS now declared (`target_job_column: dt_job`), so the reason recorded on 2026-09-04 is spent -- but turning `enabled` on makes the LOADER refuse the whole file: `allow_chain_trigger cycle: wafer_map_metadata -> dt_inventory -> wafer_map_metadata`. Measured 2026-09-06 with the committed validator, on this file and on the sample. So enabling it is blocked on the cycle ruling, not on this rule. WHY IT WENT OFF, KEPT BECAUSE THE NUMBER IS THE REASON: disabled 2026-09-04 by lead on measured cost rather than on principle -- it refused on every event, so it contributed nothing; I first left it enabled because it is alone in its trigger group and so destroys no sibling's writes. The 50-row load run falsified the other half of that: 50 input rows became 101 outbox events, and this rule turned the 51 derived ones into 153 refusal groups at three retries each, draining at 2.5-3.5s per group. The mapper itself took 0.5s; the run took 3.5 minutes. That multiplier IS the owner's 'the chain does not tick'. If it starts refusing again, THAT is the shape to look for: a refusal here multiplies into retry groups rather than failing once. See the cycle note on the standard-map rule. | S2 identity projection. This is the explicitly allowed second hop of dt_log -> wafer_map_metadata -> dt_inventory. The worker keeps all other chain-created events blocked; allow_chain_trigger is safe only because the loader rejects cycles among such edges.",
      "name": "dt_metadata_to_dt_inventory",
      "target_job_column": "dt_job",
      "trigger_table": "wafer_map_metadata",
      "target_table": "dt_inventory",
      "mapper_module": "mappers.dt_inventory_metadata_mapper",
      "mapper_function": "copy_dt_metadata_to_inventory_batch",
      "is_batch": true,
      "metadata_target_table": "dt_log",
      "allow_chain_trigger": true,
      "enabled": false
    },
    {
      "__comment": "S3: dt_inventory carries the confirmed dt_lot/dt_slot, the confirmed dt_frame and its derived portable X/Y/sign/offset equation. Project matching dt_log rows into the standard-coordinate dt_map, which is keyed by the PHYSICAL unit (dt_lot, dt_slot). | Several jobs converge on one map, so this rule RETRACTS instead of replacing: replace_map purges a whole map and would delete a sibling job's cells to correct this one's. `retract` removes only what this job owns and no longer derives, selected positively by target_job_column. | target_job_column must be DECLARED here -- dt_map now declares two map_key_columns and neither of them is the job, so the name can no longer be derived. | map_key_source_columns is omitted because dt_inventory spells the confirmed lot and slot with the same names dt_map's map key uses; declare it if a deployment differs.",
      "name": "dt_inventory_to_standard_dt_map",
      "trigger_table": "dt_inventory",
      "target_table": "dt_map",
      "source_table": "dt_log",
      "mapper_module": "mappers.dt_standard_map_mapper",
      "mapper_function": "build_standard_dt_map_batches",
      "trigger_job_column": "dt_job",
      "source_job_column": "dt_job",
      "target_job_column": "dt_job",
      "is_batch": true,
      "allow_chain_trigger": true,
      "allow_map_metadata_upsert": true,
      "allow_retraction": true,
      "enabled": false
    },
    {
      "__comment": "DISABLED 2026-09-04 by lead, to stop it deleting a sibling's writes. It refuses on every event (resolve_column against dt_inventory), so it can never contribute; but a chain group commits only if the whole group succeeds, so its raise erases what dt_log_to_dt_alignment_metadata wrote in the same dt_log group. Measured as a controlled pair: 0 rows with a raising sibling, 5 rows on the same input without one. Re-enable by declaring the job column it asks for. | Core automatic frame: dt_core_view is review-only and is intentionally NOT in this chain. The mapper reads one completed dt_log job, selects the primary core by the earliest dt_index (stable lot/slot/wafer tie breakers), filters the same raw rows in the shared alignment engine, then atomically writes dt_inventory.core_frame plus core_x/y base/sign/offset equations. All material-specific policy lives below: change reference.table/map_id_template/fields when the physical defect map becomes wafer-id keyed; change accepted_metrics and alignment_thresholds without editing Python. The configured empty index_col forces defect/bin value scoring instead of transfer-order scoring.",
      "name": "dt_log_to_primary_core_frame",
      "trigger_table": "dt_log",
      "target_table": "dt_inventory",
      "source_table": "dt_log",
      "map_table": "dt_log",
      "mapper_module": "mappers.core_alignment_mapper",
      "mapper_function": "build_core_frame_confirmation_batch",
      "is_batch": true,
      "alignment_rule": "core_frame_review",
      "target_field": "core_frame",
      "job_column": "dt_job",
      "primary_selector": {
        "group_columns": [
          "core_lot",
          "core_slot",
          "core_wafer"
        ],
        "order_columns": [
          "dt_index",
          "core_lot",
          "core_slot",
          "core_wafer"
        ]
      },
      "reference": {
        "table": "core_wafer_map",
        "map_id_template": "{core_wafer}",
        "fields": [
          "core_wafer"
        ]
      },
      "x_col": "core_x",
      "y_col": "core_y",
      "value_col": "c_bn",
      "index_col": "",
      "accepted_metrics": [
        "values",
        "occupancy"
      ],
      "assume_reference_geometry": true,
      "allow_assumed_geometry": true,
      "__alignment_thresholds_derivation": "DELIBERATE DEPARTURE from map_overlay_config.alignment (20/20) — set by the product owner, not transcribed. Read this before you delete it as an underived copy. | map_overlay_config.json's red line says 'do not transcribe these numbers, re-derive them for a different floor'. This IS the different floor, which is exactly the case that line describes: the 20 was derived against the valid_die_ref FLOOR (seed_valid_die_ref_floor.py's verify_discrimination reports that floor separates 7 non-identity frames by at least 20 dies), and this chain does not score against that floor. It scores a core wafer map (reference.table=core_wafer_map) whose reference CARRIES VALUES (value_col=c_bn), and a value-carrying reference discriminates on bin fingerprint rather than on occupancy, so the floor's occupancy resolution is not this axis's resolution. | The override is applied by alignment_view_service as a SHALLOW update on a per-invocation copy, so alignment.index (the sequence axis) keeps its own 20/20 — that key separation is deliberate and is the only thing that limited the blast radius the one time these were lowered (2026-08-05, 20 -> 1). Do not deepen that merge. | If you change reference.table or value_col, these two numbers stop being derived and must be derived again.",
      "alignment_thresholds": {
        "min_margin_dies": 5,
        "min_discriminating_dies": 10
      },
      "enabled": false
    },
    {
      "__comment": "DISABLED 2026-09-04 by lead: this rule refuses on every dt_inventory event (trigger_job_column is not declared and dt_inventory's business key is composite), so it has never run -- and because it shares a trigger group with dt_inventory_to_standard_dt_map, its raise ROLLS BACK that rule's writes. Filling the slots is NOT the safe fix: core_usage_map's map key is core_wafer, dt_inventory has no core_wafer, and the mapper emits replace_map with scope={'core_wafer': None} -- a whole-map purge with a null scope. Turning it off changes no working behaviour because nothing worked. | Usage overlay refresh after a core frame/equation update. The output is its own derived table and replaces exactly one enriched core_wafer map. This is the allowed second hop from the automatic core-frame chain.",
      "name": "dt_inventory_to_core_usage_map",
      "trigger_table": "dt_inventory",
      "target_table": "core_usage_map",
      "source_table": "dt_log",
      "inventory_table": "dt_inventory",
      "mapper_module": "mappers.core_usage_mapper",
      "mapper_function": "build_core_usage_map_batches",
      "is_batch": true,
      "allow_chain_trigger": true,
      "allow_map_metadata_upsert": true,
      "allow_replace_map": true,
      "enabled": false
    },
    {
      "__comment": "DISABLED 2026-09-04 by lead, to stop it deleting a sibling's writes. It refuses on every event (resolve_column against dt_inventory), so it can never contribute; but a chain group commits only if the whole group succeeds, so its raise erases what dt_log_to_dt_alignment_metadata wrote in the same dt_log group. Measured as a controlled pair: 0 rows with a raising sibling, 5 rows on the same input without one. Re-enable by declaring the job column it asks for. | Usage overlay revisit after raw ingest or enrichment fills core_wafer. lot/slot-only rows are intentionally a no-op; when enrichment writes core_wafer the same rule rebuilds that wafer's complete usage footprint.",
      "name": "dt_log_to_core_usage_map",
      "trigger_table": "dt_log",
      "target_table": "core_usage_map",
      "source_table": "dt_log",
      "inventory_table": "dt_inventory",
      "mapper_module": "mappers.core_usage_mapper",
      "mapper_function": "build_core_usage_map_batches",
      "is_batch": true,
      "allow_map_metadata_upsert": true,
      "allow_replace_map": true,
      "enabled": false
    },
    {
      "__comment": "[NOT fixture data -- DO NOT DELETE AS DEAD] This table is not referenced by any fixture, so it looks unused. It is not. It exists because it is the only live exercise of the CHAIN path: test_api.py::test_chained_ingestion asserts on the outbox events the production_plan -> inventory_master chain produces, and removing these declarations silently removes that coverage. The dt_log_to_dt_map fixture chain cannot replace it -- that rule ships enabled:false on purpose (a chain can upsert map cells but cannot purge them), and turning an unsafe rule on to keep a test green is the same mistake as deleting the test. Its auto-update collector is disabled, so the table is declared but never grows. | The rule stays ENABLED so the test exercises the real worker path rather than a mock.",
      "name": "production_to_inventory_reservation_batch",
      "trigger_table": "production_plan",
      "target_table": "inventory_master",
      "mapper_module": "mappers.production_mapper",
      "mapper_function": "reserve_materials_batch_df",
      "is_batch": true,
      "enabled": true,
      "__why_enabled": "SHIPS ENABLED because a test asserts on what it produces: this rule's own comment says it is the only live exercise of the CHAIN path and that test_api.py::test_chained_ingestion reads the outbox events it creates. Turning it off removes that coverage silently."
    },
    {
      "__comment": "lot_event -> lot_slot_wafer: the two parallel colon lists (slotnumbers, waferids) become one row per (lot, slot, wafer, time). Owner ruling 2026-08-31 - derive it from lot_event through the chain, the way production spreads derived tables. A view exists only on the box whose script ran, so a declaration pointing at one ships a source that never appears elsewhere. | list_delimiter IS DECLARED, NOT ASSUMED: a separator is a property of the feed, and a wrong one produces ROWS rather than an error - every wafer id glued into one - so nobody notices until a map is wrong. The five column names are the same kind of fact and are overridable for the same reason. | A row whose two lists DISAGREE is refused by the mapper, never trimmed to the shorter one: trimming drops wafers silently and the loss is indistinguishable from wafers that were never in the lot. Measured on the authoring box 2026-08-31: 80 rows carry both lists, 907 pairs, ZERO disagree. | SHIPS DISABLED because the target table has to exist first - a chain cannot create it, and it cannot write into a view. Enable this once lot_slot_wafer is a real table and the ledger source needs NO change at all: the table it already declares, business_key lot_slot_wafer_key, is exactly what this mapper builds. | Proven on the authoring box 2026-08-31, in this order: baseline 907 has_wafer atoms, drop the view, let the declared table stand in its place, enable, run the chain (907 rows, 5,442 cells, 907 distinct keys), re-translate the ledger source over its 25 lots, and compare - 907 atoms again, same predicate, same 238 {lot, slot} subjects. The count matching is not the proof on its own; the content matching is.",
      "name": "lot_event_to_lot_slot_wafer",
      "trigger_table": "lot_event",
      "target_table": "lot_slot_wafer",
      "mapper_module": "mappers.lot_slot_wafer_mapper",
      "mapper_function": "build_lot_slot_wafer_rows",
      "is_batch": true,
      "enabled": true,
      "list_delimiter": ":",
      "slot_list_column": "slotnumbers",
      "wafer_list_column": "waferids",
      "lot_column": "lot_id",
      "time_column": "event_time",
      "event_type_column": "event_type",
      "__why_enabled": "SHIPS ENABLED by owner ruling 2026-08-31, quoted in this rule's own comment: derive lot_slot_wafer from lot_event THROUGH THE CHAIN, the way production spreads derived tables. A view exists only on the box whose script ran, so shipping it off would ship a source that never appears elsewhere."
    },
    {
      "__comment": "[S-100 ⓐ] Writes one row per dt_job with that job's row count. The ledger does not aggregate (local, no computation), so the count runs here. The ledger's Python mapper `dt-job-role` used to count len(unit), and the declaration beside it pointed at dt_index, which it never read (rulings 197 · 211).",
      "name": "dt_log_to_dt_job_rollup",
      "trigger_table": "dt_log",
      "target_table": "dt_job_rollup",
      "mapper_module": "mappers.dt_job_rollup_mapper",
      "mapper_function": "build_dt_job_rollup_rows",
      "is_batch": true,
      "enabled": true,
      "__why_enabled": "SHIPS ENABLED because the shipped ledger declaration READS dt_job_rollup (measured 2026-09-10). The ledger is local and calculation-free so it cannot count a group; S-100 (a) moved that count here. Off, the ledger source has nothing to read."
    },
    {
      "__comment": "A process row carries the material type of its step: step_phase lists the DT steps, and this join copies mat_type onto every wafer_process row with that step - the ledger says the row about a dtwafer when it reads DT and about a wafer when it is blank (363db7dfa). To take a step out, empty its mat_type rather than deleting the row: a deleted source row names no key, so the rows it filled keep their value.",
      "name": "step_phase_to_wafer_process",
      "__why_enabled": "SHIPS ENABLED because the shipped ledger declaration READS wafer_process.mat_type (363db7dfa, lead 1dd4091a6): wafer_process_recipe says its sentence about a dtwafer when that cell is DT, and this join is its only writer. With step_phase empty it writes nothing, so every row stays on the core wafer as before.",
      "on": {
        "table": "step_phase"
      },
      "derive": {
        "kind": "join",
        "join": {
          "on": [
            {
              "left": "step",
              "right": "step"
            }
          ],
          "take": [
            "mat_type"
          ]
        }
      },
      "into": {
        "table": "wafer_process"
      },
      "key": {
        "unique": true
      }
    },
    {
      "__comment": "Unified declaration ① join — when a value in on.table (the source) changes, the take columns are written to the row with the same key in into.table. In join.on, left is a column of the into table and right a column of the source. TWO rules stand up: this name (when the source changes) and name:target (when a row enters the into table or its key changes). The source must be unique per key, and key.unique declares that.",
      "name": "sample_unified_join",
      "enabled": false,
      "on": {
        "table": "dt_job_attribution"
      },
      "derive": {
        "kind": "join",
        "join": {
          "on": [
            {
              "left": "dt_job",
              "right": "dt_job"
            }
          ],
          "take": [
            "dt_lot_confirmed",
            "dt_slot_confirmed"
          ]
        }
      },
      "into": {
        "table": "dt_inventory"
      },
      "key": {
        "unique": true
      }
    },
    {
      "__comment": "Unified declaration ③ derive — makes a place where a person CHOOSES. The reference_views SQL makes the candidates, and candidate_for says WHICH column they are candidates for. auto_confirm=false, so a person confirms. ⚠️ This one declaration stands up TWO rules (derive + the auto-confirm seat) — the seat stands even with auto-confirm off.",
      "name": "sample_unified_decide",
      "enabled": false,
      "on": {
        "table": "dt_log"
      },
      "into": {
        "table": "dt_inventory"
      },
      "derive": {
        "kind": "decide",
        "decide": {
          "key": [
            "dt_job"
          ],
          "fields": [
            "dt_lot",
            "dt_slot"
          ],
          "auto_confirm": false,
          "alignment": true,
          "reference_views": [
            {
              "label": "Observed dt_lot / dt_slot — what this job says",
              "query": "SELECT COALESCE(dt_lot,'') AS dt_lot, COALESCE(dt_slot,'') AS dt_slot, COUNT(*) AS cells FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job GROUP BY 1, 2 ORDER BY cells DESC",
              "limit": 50,
              "candidate_for": {
                "dt_lot": "dt_lot",
                "dt_slot": "dt_slot"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "__comment": "Unified declaration ③ mapper — attaches a mapper the operator wrote in server/mappers/. mapper is the NAME that file registers with @mapper, and params are read by that mapper.",
      "name": "sample_unified_mapper",
      "enabled": false,
      "on": {
        "table": "dt_log"
      },
      "into": {
        "table": "dt_log"
      },
      "derive": {
        "kind": "mapper",
        "mapper": {
          "mapper": "my_operator_mapper",
          "params": {
            "note": "Value the mapper reads"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "__comment": "Unified declaration ④ auto-confirm — the SAME grammar as ③ with only auto_confirm true. With ONE candidate it confirms without a person; with two or more it leaves them to a person. ⚠️ So auto-confirm is not a separate declaration but ONE field of the derive declaration.",
      "name": "sample_unified_auto_confirm",
      "enabled": false,
      "on": {
        "table": "dt_log"
      },
      "into": {
        "table": "dt_inventory"
      },
      "derive": {
        "kind": "decide",
        "decide": {
          "key": [
            "dt_job"
          ],
          "fields": [
            "dt_lot",
            "dt_slot"
          ],
          "auto_confirm": true,
          "alignment": true,
          "reference_views": [
            {
              "label": "Observed dt_lot / dt_slot — what this job says",
              "query": "SELECT COALESCE(dt_lot,'') AS dt_lot, COALESCE(dt_slot,'') AS dt_slot, COUNT(*) AS cells FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job GROUP BY 1, 2 ORDER BY cells DESC",
              "limit": 50,
              "candidate_for": {
                "dt_lot": "dt_lot",
                "dt_slot": "dt_slot"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "__comment": "3a262cc76 - a saved contrast: a contrast_run row -> the walk's ranked candidates as contrast_factor rows, by the tracked mapper server/mappers/contrast_walk.py. The ranking is the walk route's own (GET /api/ledger/subgraph with the run's arguments); until binds the walk, so a replay writes the same rows. enabled: false switches it off.",
      "name": "contrast_factor_from_run",
      "__why_enabled": "SHIPS ENABLED because the R&D board's Save contrast writes contrast_run and reads contrast_factor (3a262cc76, owner 09-30): off, a saved contrast is a row nothing computes. It runs only when a contrast_run row is written.",
      "enabled": true,
      "is_batch": true,
      "on": {
        "table": "contrast_run"
      },
      "into": {
        "table": "contrast_factor"
      },
      "derive": {
        "kind": "mapper",
        "mapper": {
          "mapper_module": "mappers.contrast_walk",
          "mapper_function": "contrast_walk"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "__comment": "Every bind param in a reference view MUST be a decision_key column -- the loader rejects anything else (enrichment_config.py:126-129) and drops just that view. A view that binds only PART of the decision key is legal but gets flagged scope_unresolved, because one confirmed value would then be stamped onto every member of the wider scope; both rules in this file bind the FULL key. | candidate_for is {target_field: view_result_column}. Without it declaring_views is empty, resolve_target_candidate refuses before running any SQL (enrichment_candidates.py:306-308), and the probe emits ZERO log lines -- which is indistinguishable from 'the feature is off'. That silent state is what this config exists to avoid. | INFERENCE #1 -- confirm which DT lot and slot a DT job's wafer ended up as. This cannot be done with a SQL join: bonding_log's (dt_lot, dt_slot) is the value AS OF BONDING and dt_log's is the value AS OF DT, and split/merge moved the wafer in between, so a naive join does not error -- it matches the wrong wafer plausibly. | auto_confirm is ON here: the candidate is a RECORDED FACT (an equipment track-in event) plus an assignment fixed by the monotonic constraint, not a guess, and a single distinct answer is the only thing that confirms. Anything else -- zero candidates, two candidates, a failed view -- stays in the queue for a human.",
      "name": "dt_job_lot_slot_attribution",
      "enabled": false,
      "on": {
        "table": "dt_log"
      },
      "into": {
        "table": "dt_job_attribution"
      },
      "derive": {
        "kind": "decide",
        "decide": {
          "key": [
            "dt_job"
          ],
          "fields": [
            "dt_lot_confirmed",
            "dt_slot_confirmed"
          ],
          "list_columns": [
            "cell_count"
          ],
          "aggregations": {
            "cell_count": "count"
          },
          "auto_confirm": true,
          "reference_views": [
            {
              "label": "이 job의 원본 셀",
              "query": "SELECT dt_eqp, product, dt_lot, dt_slot, dt_x, dt_y, core_wafer, core_lot, core_slot, c_bn, event_time FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job ORDER BY dt_y, dt_x",
              "limit": 400
            },
            {
              "label": "기록된 dt_lot/dt_slot (10%는 틀린 값이다)",
              "query": "SELECT COALESCE(dt_lot, '') AS dt_lot, COALESCE(dt_slot, '') AS dt_slot, COUNT(*) AS cells FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job GROUP BY 1, 2 ORDER BY cells DESC",
              "limit": 50
            },
            {
              "label": "앵커 - core_wafer가 적힌 셀",
              "query": "SELECT core_wafer, core_lot, core_slot, COUNT(*) AS dies FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job AND COALESCE(core_wafer, '') <> '' GROUP BY 1, 2, 3 ORDER BY dies DESC",
              "limit": 100
            },
            {
              "label": "설비 track-in이 말하는 DT 랏",
              "query": "WITH j AS (  SELECT MIN(dt_eqp) AS eqp, MIN(event_time) AS et  FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job) SELECT DISTINCT le.lot AS dt_lot FROM lot_event le, j WHERE le.event_type = 'track_in' AND le.equipment = j.eqp AND le.event_time = j.et",
              "limit": 50,
              "candidate_for": {
                "dt_lot_confirmed": "dt_lot"
              }
            },
            {
              "label": "단조 배정이 말하는 DT 슬롯",
              "query": "WITH j AS (  SELECT MIN(dt_eqp) AS eqp, MIN(event_time) AS et,         MIN(split_part(dt_job, '_', 3)) AS tape_slot  FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job), peers AS (  SELECT DISTINCT split_part(d.dt_job, '_', 3) AS tape_slot  FROM dt_log d, j WHERE d.dt_eqp = j.eqp AND d.event_time = j.et), rk AS (  SELECT COUNT(*) AS pos FROM peers, j WHERE peers.tape_slot <= j.tape_slot), ti AS (  SELECT le.slot_numbers AS slots FROM lot_event le, j  WHERE le.event_type = 'track_in' AND le.equipment = j.eqp AND le.event_time = j.et) SELECT (string_to_array(ti.slots, ':'))[rk.pos] AS dt_slot FROM ti, rk",
              "limit": 50,
              "candidate_for": {
                "dt_slot_confirmed": "dt_slot"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "dt_frame_confrimation",
      "enabled": false,
      "on": {
        "table": "dt_log"
      },
      "into": {
        "table": "dt_inventory"
      },
      "derive": {
        "kind": "decide",
        "decide": {
          "key": [
            "dt_job"
          ],
          "fields": [
            "dt_frame"
          ],
          "list_columns": [],
          "aggregations": {},
          "auto_confirm": false,
          "reference_views": [
            {
              "label": "이 job 의 원본 행 (dt_log)",
              "query": "SELECT dt_eqp, product, dt_lot, dt_slot, dt_x, dt_y, c_bn, dt_index, event_time FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job ORDER BY dt_y, dt_x",
              "limit": 400
            },
            {
              "label": "관측된 좌표 범위 — 프레임이 덮어야 하는 것",
              "query": "SELECT MIN(dt_x) AS x_min, MAX(dt_x) AS x_max, MIN(dt_y) AS y_min, MAX(dt_y) AS y_max, COUNT(*) AS cells, COUNT(DISTINCT c_bn) AS bins FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job",
              "limit": 1
            },
            {
              "label": "같은 장비의 다른 job 들 — 프레임이 대개 같다",
              "query": "SELECT dt_job, MIN(dt_eqp) AS dt_eqp, COUNT(*) AS cells, MIN(event_time) AS first_seen FROM dt_log WHERE dt_eqp = (SELECT MIN(dt_eqp) FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job) GROUP BY dt_job ORDER BY first_seen DESC",
              "limit": 50
            }
          ],
          "alignment": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "__comment": "Manual visual review only. dt_core_view is a separate raw core-coordinate map namespace for each DT job; its source is deliberately not dt_log because dt_log/dt_job already owns the DT-frame wafer_map_metadata record. This rule proposes no automatic conclusion and must remain auto_confirm=false until core bin-fingerprint/topology evidence has an agreed acceptance policy.",
      "name": "core_frame_review",
      "enabled": false,
      "on": {
        "table": "dt_core_view"
      },
      "into": {
        "table": "dt_inventory"
      },
      "derive": {
        "kind": "decide",
        "decide": {
          "key": [
            "dt_job"
          ],
          "fields": [
            "core_frame"
          ],
          "list_columns": [],
          "aggregations": {},
          "auto_confirm": false,
          "reference_views": [
            {
              "label": "이 job 의 core 원본 행 (dt_core_view)",
              "query": "SELECT core_lot, core_slot, core_wafer, core_x, core_y, c_bn, core_index FROM dt_core_view WHERE dt_job = :dt_job ORDER BY core_y, core_x",
              "limit": 400
            },
            {
              "label": "관측된 core 좌표 범위 — 프레임이 덮어야 하는 것",
              "query": "SELECT MIN(core_x) AS x_min, MAX(core_x) AS x_max, MIN(core_y) AS y_min, MAX(core_y) AS y_max, COUNT(*) AS cells, COUNT(DISTINCT core_wafer) AS wafers FROM dt_core_view WHERE dt_job = :dt_job",
              "limit": 1
            },
            {
              "label": "이 job 이 쓴 core 웨이퍼들",
              "query": "SELECT core_wafer, core_lot, core_slot, COUNT(*) AS dies FROM dt_core_view WHERE dt_job = :dt_job AND COALESCE(core_wafer,'') <> '' GROUP BY 1,2,3 ORDER BY dies DESC",
              "limit": 100
            }
          ],
          "alignment": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "__comment": "dt_inventory 의 dt_lot/dt_slot 은 401 중 400 이 비어 있다. dt_log 가 같은 job 에 대해 그 쌍을 이미 들고 있으므로, 사람은 «고르는» 것이 아니라 «확인»한다. 두 대상을 한 뷰에 선언 순서로 담아 candidate_for 가 열 순서를 말할 수 있게 한다.",
      "name": "dt_lot_slot_from_log",
      "enabled": false,
      "on": {
        "table": "dt_log"
      },
      "into": {
        "table": "dt_inventory"
      },
      "derive": {
        "kind": "decide",
        "decide": {
          "key": [
            "dt_job"
          ],
          "fields": [
            "dt_lot",
            "dt_slot"
          ],
          "list_columns": [],
          "aggregations": {},
          "auto_confirm": false,
          "alignment": true,
          "reference_views": [
            {
              "label": "관측된 dt_lot / dt_slot — 이 job 이 말하는 것",
              "query": "SELECT COALESCE(dt_lot,'') AS dt_lot, COALESCE(dt_slot,'') AS dt_slot, COUNT(*) AS cells FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job GROUP BY 1, 2 ORDER BY cells DESC",
              "limit": 50,
              "candidate_for": {
                "dt_lot": "dt_lot",
                "dt_slot": "dt_slot"
              }
            },
            {
              "label": "이 job 의 원본 행 (근거)",
              "query": "SELECT dt_eqp, product, dt_lot, dt_slot, dt_x, dt_y, c_bn, event_time FROM dt_log WHERE dt_job = :dt_job ORDER BY dt_y, dt_x",
              "limit": 400
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}